Quoting  RjY  <rjyATusersDOTsourceforgeDOTnet>:
>Martin Read posted:
>>You don't need MC3 in Spork or vanilla, [...]
>Could you or David explain why this is? I am under the impression that
>magic cancellation protects you from so many irritating special types of
>attack (draining and sliming being two obvious ones) that I can't see
>why anybody would want to go without the highest level of it.

"Demogorgon strikes at your displaced image and misses you!" is one reason
why.

Sliming is a joke - slimes are slow and it is trivially cured with
resources available when slimes turn up.

Draining can be a nuisance, but it doesn't become a threat until the point
when you aren't going to gain levels by killing monsters anyway - so as
long as you can arrange to kill something (anything) after a successful
drain but before another successful drain, you take no penalty. Draining
doesn't work that often anyway, and of course displacement provides
another layer of defence.

If you're going to rush into the middle of a group of vampires screaming
"suck me like the last mint in the packet!", sure, MC3. But if you're
going to be disciplined about tackling drainers with a bit of caution -
exactly the sort of caution that is valuable anyway - you're not going to
permanently lose levels with MC2.

Also, there's no rule against swapping in a particular cloak (or weapon)
for a particularly troublesome situation. That doesn't mean you need to
cruise around with MC3 all the time, anymore than you have to wear oilskin
all the time because your strategy for Water involves it.

>tend to fixate on wizards). At a total guess I suppose it isn't
>unreasonable that with invisibility and displacement most of the attacks
>that would get through MC2 would in fact miss, but I'm not convinced
>it's worth the risk.

Invisibility is not an asset once you have displacement. 
